matches:
    "statement": "Let $\mathcal{Q}(n)$ be the $n$-Queens' graph, let $\sigma(\mathcal{Q}(n))$ denote its spectrum, and let $\mathbb{Z}$ denote the integers. **Integer-spectrum conjecture.** For $n\geq4$,

$$
\sigma(\mathcal{Q}(n))\cap\mathbb{Z}=\begin{cases}
\left\{-4,n-4\right\},&\text{if $n$ is even;}\\
\left\{-4,-3,\ldots,\frac{n-11}{2}\right\}\cup\left\{\frac{n-5}{2},\ldots,n-5,n-4\right\},&\text{if $n$ is odd.}
\end{cases}
$$

Furthermore, when $n$ is even, the eigenvalue $n-4$ has multiplicity $(n-2)/2$; when $n$ is odd, the eigenvalue $n-4$ has multiplicity $(n+1)/2$, and the eigenvalues $-3,-2,\dots,\frac{n-11}{2},\frac{n-5}{2},\dots,n-6,n-5$ are simple. The claim is motivated by computations for several values of $n$; the source gives no resolution, so its status remains open.",

    "literal_statement": "Let $\mathcal{Q}(n)$ denote the $n$-Queens graph, and let $\sigma(\mathcal{Q}(n))$ be its set of distinct eigenvalues. For $n\teq 4$, consider the integers in

$$
\sigma(\mathcal{Q}(n)) \cap \mathbb{Z} \supseteq
\begin{cases}
\left\{ -4, n-4 \right\}, & \text{if $n$ is even;} \\
\left\{ -4, -3, \ldots, \frac{n-11}{2} \right\} \cup \left\{ \frac{n-5}{2}, \ldots, n-5, n-4 \right\}, & \text{if $n$ is odd.}
\end{cases}
$$

**Integer-eigenvalue conjecture.** For $n \geq 4$, these are the unique integer eigenvalues of $\mathcal{Q}(n)$. If $n$ is even, $n-4$ has multiplicity $\frac{n-2}{2}$; if $n$ is odd, it has multiplicity $\frac{n+1}{2}$. The eigenvalues $-3,-2,\dots,\frac{n-11}{2},\frac{n-5}{2},\dots,n-6,n-5$ are simple. The claim is motivated by computations for several values of $n$; the exact characterization of the integer eigenvalues remains open.",
